<section xml:id="tryit-xml">
    <title>Theorem 3.2.6</title>

    <proof>
        <p>
        <me>
            \text{Consider three parallel lines } l_1, l_2, \text{ and } l_3
            \text{ intersected by a transversal } t_1
            \text{ such that } t_1 \text{ is divided into two congruent sections.}
        </me>
        </p>
        <p>
        <me>
            \text{If } t_1 \text{ is perpendicular to the parallel lines, then the
            shortest distance between the neighboring lines is equal.}
        </me>
        </p>
        <p>
        <me>
            \text{Now consider the case of } t_1 \text{ not being perpendicular.
            Then using alternate interior angles, the angle formed by } t_1
            \text{ and } l_1 \text{ is equal to the angle formed by } t_1
            \text{ and } l_3.
            \text{ We can draw the perpendicular from the intersection of } t_1
            \text{ and } l_2 \text{ that spans from } l_1 \text{ to } l_3.
            \text{ There now exist two triangles, one between } l_1 \text{ and } l_2
            \text{ and one between } l_2 \text{ and } l_3.
        </me>
        </p>
        <p>
        <me>
            \text{The hypotenuse of each is equal as given, and the two corresponding
            angles are equal as shown with alternate interior angles and the definition
            of perpendicular. Hence these two triangles are congruent.
            Considering corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent,
            the two sets of parallels } l_1 l_2 \text{ and } l_2 l_3
            \text{ are equidistant.}
        </me>
        </p>
        <p>
        <me>
            \text{Now consider some other transversal } t_2.
            \text{ Draw the perpendicular from } l_1 \text{ to } l_3
            \text{ through the } t_2 l_2 \text{ intersection.
            In accordance with the parallel postulate, these perpendicular segments
            are congruent. As they are perpendicular, each segment has a right angle
            on one end, and the other end is shown to be congruent through the vertical
            angles. Hence there are congruent triangles once again.}
        </me>
        </p>
        <p>
        <me>
            \text{Considering corresponding parts of congruent triangles are congruent,
            this arbitrary transversal is divided into congruent segments by the three
            parallel lines.}
        </me>
        </p>
        <image src="paralell.svg">
    </proof>
</section>
